WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.779
Over ten episodes, we're going to take apart one of the biggest, most important, and most confusing systems in the state: public education.

2
00:00:08.779 --> 00:00:21.283
California enrolls somewhere around six million students in its public schools, taught by something like three hundred twenty thousand teachers, spread across roughly eleven thousand public schools.

3
00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:24.188
That's more kids than many states have people.

4
00:00:24.188 --> 00:00:31.009
If you're a parent, a teacher, a voter, or just someone who cares, this series is meant to give you the map.

5
00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:33.194
Let's start with a blunt question.

6
00:00:33.194 --> 00:00:35.443
How are California's schools doing?

7
00:00:35.443 --> 00:00:39.363
The honest answer is: it depends on what you compare them to.

8
00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:49.837
On the national test known as NAEP, sometimes called "the Nation's Report Card," California has for years landed near the middle of the pack, often a bit below it.

9
00:00:49.837 --> 00:00:57.934
But California also serves an unusually high share of students who are learning English and students from low-income families.

10
00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:08.345
When researchers adjust for those differences, the picture shifts, and some groups of California students do as well as or better than similar students elsewhere.

11
00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:13.999
Zoom out further and you can compare the whole United States to other wealthy countries.

12
00:01:13.999 --> 00:01:27.494
On international tests, American students actually score above the average of other wealthy countries in reading and science, and close to the average in math — not clearly below it, as people sometimes assume.

13
00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:29.743
What stands out isn't that the U.S.

14
00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:31.543
is failing across the board.

15
00:01:31.543 --> 00:01:36.555
It's that the gap between our highest and lowest performers is unusually wide.

16
00:01:36.555 --> 00:01:38.392
Why does any of this matter?

17
00:01:38.392 --> 00:01:40.491
Because the economy has changed.

18
00:01:40.491 --> 00:01:45.935
A century ago, you could build a solid life on physical work and a basic education.

19
00:01:45.935 --> 00:01:56.101
Today, the jobs that pay well increasingly reward the ability to read closely, reason with numbers, solve unfamiliar problems, and learn new tools quickly.

20
00:01:56.101 --> 00:01:58.987
Artificial intelligence is speeding that up.

21
00:01:58.987 --> 00:02:06.858
When software can do routine tasks, the human skills that remain valuable are judgment, communication, and adaptability.

22
00:02:06.858 --> 00:02:10.728
Those are exactly the skills schools are supposed to build.

23
00:02:10.728 --> 00:02:15.385
And when schools fall short, the costs don't stay inside the classroom.

24
00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:27.520
Adults without a high school diploma earn less on average, pay less in taxes, are more likely to rely on public assistance, and are more likely to end up in the criminal justice system.

25
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:36.834
Economists have tried to put a dollar figure on this, and the estimates run into hundreds of thousands of dollars per student over a lifetime.

26
00:02:36.834 --> 00:02:40.245
Education isn't only a private good for the student.

27
00:02:40.245 --> 00:02:42.737
It's a shared investment for everyone.

28
00:02:42.737 --> 00:02:44.136
Here's a tricky part.

29
00:02:44.136 --> 00:02:51.862
Most parents think their own child's school is doing fine, even when they believe schools in general are struggling.

30
00:02:51.862 --> 00:02:58.723
Psychologists have a name for this kind of optimism, and it shows up everywhere, not just in education.

31
00:02:58.723 --> 00:03:00.455
It's reinforced by grades.

32
00:03:00.455 --> 00:03:10.113
Grades have crept upward over the decades, so a report card full of A's and B's doesn't always mean a student is on track for college-level work.

33
00:03:10.113 --> 00:03:22.436
One of the best habits a parent can build is to look at more than one signal: grades, yes, but also state test results, teacher conversations, and the actual work the child brings home.

34
00:03:22.436 --> 00:03:24.434
So are schools getting better?

35
00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:25.966
Over the long run, yes.

36
00:03:25.966 --> 00:03:27.965
Graduation rates have climbed.

37
00:03:27.965 --> 00:03:30.363
More students take advanced courses.

38
00:03:30.363 --> 00:03:35.558
Scores for younger students rose meaningfully between the 1990s and the 2010s.

39
00:03:35.558 --> 00:03:43.818
Then the pandemic hit, and learning dropped sharply, especially in math and especially for students who were already behind.

40
00:03:43.818 --> 00:03:48.880
Recovery has been real but uneven, and in many places it's still incomplete.

41
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.214
To understand where we are, it helps to know where we came from.

42
00:03:53.214 --> 00:04:05.675
American public schools grew out of a nineteenth-century idea, championed by reformers like Horace Mann, that a democracy needs educated citizens and that the public should pay for it.

43
00:04:05.675 --> 00:04:19.151
The system expanded to include high schools, then kindergarten, then, after long struggles, students it had excluded: Black students, students with disabilities, students who don't yet speak English.

44
00:04:19.151 --> 00:04:22.605
Some of those struggles have names you'd recognize.

45
00:04:22.605 --> 00:04:25.923
In 1954, the Supreme Court's decision in Brown v.

46
00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:29.106
Board of Education outlawed school segregation.

47
00:04:29.106 --> 00:04:37.977
In 1972, a law called Title IX opened the door to equal opportunity for girls and women in schools, in the classroom and beyond it.

48
00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:45.630
In 1975, what we now call IDEA guaranteed students with disabilities a public education built around their needs.

49
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:54.230
And in 1982, the Supreme Court ruled that undocumented children have the same right to attend public school as any other child.

50
00:04:54.230 --> 00:05:02.763
Each of these expansions came with new laws, new court decisions, and new debates that in some cases are still going on today.

51
00:05:02.763 --> 00:05:07.571
Which brings us to the deepest question: what are schools actually for?

52
00:05:07.571 --> 00:05:09.603
People give different answers.

53
00:05:09.603 --> 00:05:11.837
Some say preparing kids for jobs.

54
00:05:11.837 --> 00:05:14.479
Some say preparing them to be citizens.

55
00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:25.382
Others say helping each child discover what they're capable of, or passing on a shared culture, or giving every kid a fair shot regardless of where they started.

56
00:05:25.382 --> 00:05:46.036
When researchers have actually put that question to people directly, the answers split roughly three ways in close to equal measure: about a third point to academic preparation, about a third to preparing kids for jobs and careers, and about a third to preparing citizens who can take part in a democracy.

57
00:05:46.036 --> 00:05:56.262
Schools try to do all of these at once, and a lot of the arguments you hear about education are really arguments about which purpose should come first.

58
00:05:56.262 --> 00:06:00.621
Over the next nine episodes, we'll follow a simple sentence.

59
00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:10.428
Education is students, teachers, time, places, and the right stuff to learn, organized into a system, with resources, aimed at success.

60
00:06:10.428 --> 00:06:12.462
Then we'll ask: so now what?

61
00:06:12.462 --> 00:06:20.671
If you'd like to go deeper, Chapter 1 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org covers these topics lesson by lesson.
